Q: Calling interface method causes stack overflow error A: <p>Interfaces should have no implementation (though they can, which has some debate whether that's ultimately good, or breaking their purpose being shoehorned into the role of a base class), so what you are doing is defining the implementation as a self-calling infinite recursion, meaning a Stack Overflow. Parent is irrelevant in this example:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public class Child : IInterface
{
    public void InterfaceMethod()
    {
        ((IInterface)this).InterfaceMethod();
        // exactly the same as:
        InterfaceMethod();
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>What Child's implementation of InterfaceMethod <em>should</em> do, is what you want the method to do &quot;as a Child&quot;. So the implementation of InterfaceMethod in child should be the implementation logic of what Child should do, not attempting to &quot;call&quot; itself again through the interface. Where this might be confused is if your child interface implementation wanted to call something from Parent (including parent.InterfaceMethod if Parent also implements the same interface. However, in that case it would just be:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>    public void InterfaceMethod()
    {
        base.InterfaceMethod(); // to call Parent (base class) 
           // .InterfaceMethod() or any other public/protected method.
    }
</code></pre>
<p>If you do want to use default implementations in the interface, and have the Child revert to the default implementation, then you would remove the method declaration in Child entirely. This would fall back to the default declared in the interface.</p>
<p>Interfaces generally serve two purposes: 1) A contract, and 2) Polymorphism. A contract interface is still polymorphic, but intended to have one &quot;real&quot; implementation. It defines what the implementation will do &quot;as&quot; the contracted object, with the intention being that the real object can be easily swapped out by a Mock for things like testing purposes. A polymorphic interface is where you have multiple unique implementations that share the same contract and can be interchanged with one another. I.e. the classic <code>interface IAnimal.MakeNoise()</code>, with <code>Dog:IAnimal</code> and <code>Cat:IAnimal</code>.</p>
<p>In both cases it is up to the concrete classes to implement the interface methods. Interfaces are not the same as base classes where if Child extends Parent and in a method in Child you want to call a method in parent, you use <code>base.ParentMethod()</code>.</p>
<p>The situation where you would use <code>((IInterface)this).InterfaceMethod();</code> is in situations where you use explicit interfaces. Explicit interfaces are a mechanism where you guard interface implementations to <em>only</em> be accessible via the interface, not concrete instances. For example:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public interface IAnimal
{
    string MakeNoise();
}

public class Dog : IAnimal
{
    string IAnimal.MakeNoise()
    {
        return &quot;Woof!&quot;;
    }

    // instead of the public implementation
    /*
    public string MakeNoise()
    {
        return &quot;Woof!&quot;;
    }
   */

    public string SomethingSpecificToDog()
    {
        // .. Dog specific logic, but we want to access MakeNoise:
        string noise = ((IAnimal)this).MakeNoise();
        // ...
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>A simple utility property I use to facilitate explicit interface access is:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public class Dog : IAnimal
{
    private IAnimal ithis =&gt; this; // or other preferred naming like &quot;AsAnimal&quot; or &quot;AsContract&quot;

    // ...


    public string SomethingSpecificToDog()
    {
        // .. Dog specific logic, but we want to access MakeNoise:
        string noise = ithis.MakeNoise();
        // ...
    }

</code></pre>
<p>Which makes accessing explicit contract methods from within each other or other code much easier. If using multiple interfaces you'd need more explicit naming to use such as <code>iAnimalThis</code> but this is most commonly in single contract interface implementations.</p>
<p>Here with explicit interfaces, if we use <code>var dog = new Dog();</code> we cannot call <code>string noise = dog.MakeNoise()</code> because that is explicitly implemented. Think of it something similar to how <code>protected</code> works.  We would have to use either <code>IAnimal dog = new Dog();</code> or <code>var dog = (IAnimal) new Dog();</code> to access <code>MakeNoise()</code>. (or <code>var dog = new Dog(); string noise = ((IAnimal) dog).MakeNoise();</code>)  The advantage of explicit implementation is in contract interfaces. If interfaces are implemented with public methods then you change an interface to remove a method, the public implementations become just regular public methods and effectively dead code. With explicit implementation you get a compiler error in that scenario which signals the code needs to be cleaned up. It also promotes ensuring that developers use the concrete instances through their interfaces, such as ensuring IoC is employed with dependencies with code accessing through the interface rather than code new'ing up concrete instances.</p>
